From 96d430503ff47682cd4e7c3cabc3b495c7fb162f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 06:55:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: scan a computed program's literal
 code and sibling scripts

The computed-shell-input refusal also fired on ordinary agent commands. In a
scan-only replay of 12,624 historical commands it newly refused 67, among them
`$PY -c '...'` with `PY=$(command -v python3)`, the `hermes-core-python`
wrapper, `scripts/run_tests.sh` and `source ./activate`.

Why: any computed program word followed by `-c` was treated as a possible shell
with unknown input, and any script operand containing a substitution was
unknown, although only the program or the leading directory was computed.

Change (cron/lifecycle_guard_substitution_output.py):
- Computed program with literal options and literal `-c` code, no stdin feed,
  no descriptor other than stdout/stderr, and no `$` or backtick left in the
  code: the code is scanned as shell text instead of refused.
- `. "<output>/literal/tail"` and `bash "<output>/literal/tail"`: the tail is
  resolved against the walk's directory and that file is scanned. A tail with
  `..`, a glob, an expansion, or naming no file there stays refused.
- `/abs/dir/<output>` as a program with no argument, pipe or stdin binding is
  not a shell invocation (`account="/Users/$(id -un)"` inside a certified
  quote).

Still refused: `eval "$(tool env)"` and every case where the parent can still
change the code, the options or a descriptor.

Known limit: the computed directory is assumed to be the walk's directory. A
prefix that evaluates elsewhere is not followed, as before this check existed.

Proof: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_computed_program.py runs real bash
against a fake hermes marker for 24 refusals and 8 allowed clean commands. The
allowed rows fail without this change (7 of the first 7 measured); the refusal
rows pass before and after.
